<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/intermediate/plant-biology">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Plants are <strong>stationary chemists</strong>: rooted in place, they solve every problem
    animals solve with movement — finding food, water, light, mates and defence — using growth
    and chemistry instead. Understanding plants is understanding how a fixed organism wins, which
    also makes them the foundation of nearly every food chain and of civilisation itself.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the plant body, system by system" widget="equationStep" config={plantCfg} />

  <h2>What makes plants remarkable</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>They build themselves from air:</strong> ~95% of a tree's dry mass is CO₂ and water pulled from air and soil. Forests are solidified atmosphere.</li>
    <li><strong>Water up a 100-metre tree with no pump:</strong> evaporation tension plus hydrogen bonding (the cohesion–tension theory) — physics working inside every leaf.</li>
    <li><strong>Plant defence is chemical warfare:</strong> caffeine, nicotine, tannins, latex — poisons, glues and alarms. Many medicines (aspirin, morphine, quinine, taxol) are plant defence chemistry borrowed.</li>
    <li><strong>They communicate:</strong> damaged acacias release airborne signals neighbours respond to; mycorrhizal networks shuttle nutrients between trees ("the wood wide web" — real, though early popular claims were oversold).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Taiz & Zeiger, Plant Physiology; OpenStax, Biology 2e (Plant Structure and Function); Wohlleben, The Hidden Life of Trees (popular, with caveats).</p>
</TopicPage>
